  "query_preview": "(Today through Sunday)...\nIssued at 237 AM EST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nBroad ridging aloft and at the surface will settle across the Ohio \nValley this week...resulting in an extended period of quiet weather \nwith temperatures warming as the week progresses. The next threat \nfor rain will not come until the weekend as a cold front moves into \nthe region.\n\nThrough Tuesday Night\n\nThe persistent stratus that lingered across the eastern half of the \nforecast area into the evening has almost finally dissipated with \njust a small area lingering over parts of Decatur and Rush Counties. \nWith clear skies and light winds over the rest of the area...patchy \nfog has developed. 07Z temperatures ranged from the upper 20s to the \nmid 30s. \n\nThere remains some potential for small pockets of stratus to linger \nor drift westward from its current position through daybreak as low \nlevel flow transitions to light E/NE. Additionally...patchy fog is \nlikely to expand somewhat in coverage through daybreak focused \nespecially across northern portions of the forecast area. Fog will \ndiminish quickly after sunrise.\n\nThe strong surface high will park itself across the region over the \nnext few days as the upper ridge axis shifts east into the Ohio \nValley. While scattered cu cannot be entirely ruled out across \neastern counties through the first half of today...an increase in \nsubsidence through the column will promote mainly sunny skies today \nand Tuesday. Surface winds will shift to southerly through Tuesday \nwith a gradual warming trend commencing. Low level thermals support \nhighs in the lower to mid 50s today and in the mid and upper 50s \nTuesday.\n\nWednesday through Sunday\n\nStrong ridging at the surface and aloft will remain across the \neastern half of the country through the rest of the work week... \nmaintaining pleasant mid Fall weather across the Ohio Valley. Deep \nsubsidence will largely continue through late Friday with clear \nskies. Increasing warm advection will enable daytime highs into \nthe 60s Wednesday through Friday with thermal profiles suggestive \nthat locations in the Wabash Valley may make a run at 70 degrees \nFriday afternoon.\n\nAn amplifying upper level trough across the Intermountain West by \nlate week will progress east into the Mississippi Valley by Saturday \nnight forcing the ridge into the western Atlantic Ocean. This will \nfinally enable a cold front to track into the region for the second \nhalf of the weekend but with ensembles suggestive of the associated \nsurface wave tracking well to the north of the region...showers are \nlikely to remain scattered in coverage along the boundary. \nTemperatures will remain warm on Saturday with temperatures sliding \nback closer to normal Sunday into early next week in the post \nfrontal airmass.",
